static const char nx_str_931[573] = "Q: Which addition wraps around on overflow instead of trapping?\n- +\n* +%\n- +|\n> `+%` wraps, `+|` saturates at the type's limits, and plain `+` traps with a defined panic.\n\nQ: What is the type of `x` in `let x = 7`?\n- i32\n* i64\n- usize\n> An integer literal takes the type the context asks for; with no context it defaults to `i64`.\n\nQ: What does `let s = \"camp\"` hold?\n- an owned String\n* a view of four bytes in the executable, a `[]u8`\n- a `char` array\n> A string literal is a slice of bytes that live in the program; `String.from(\"camp\")` would copy them onto the heap.\n";
static const char nx_str_932[503] = "Q: What does `try f()` do when `f` returns an error?\n- panics with the error's name\n* returns that error from the current function\n- turns the error into `null`\n> `try` hands the error to the caller, whose return type must allow it; `catch` is the form that handles it here.\n\nQ: When does a `defer` statement run?\n- right away\n- only when the function returns an error\n* when the enclosing scope ends, however it ends\n> `defer` runs at scope exit on every path; `errdefer` runs only on the error path.\n";
static const char nx_str_933[762] = "Q: After `let b = a` where `a` is a `List(i32)`, what is `a`?\n* moved: using it is a compile error\n- a copy of `b`\n- an alias of `b`\n> Owning values move on assignment; `.clone()` is the explicit copy.\n\nQ: A function takes `xs: List(String)` without `own`. What may it do with `xs`?\n* read it\n- move it into a struct\n- drop it early\n> Parameters are borrowed: the callee reads, the caller keeps the value. `own` hands it over.\n\nQ: Which view does the compiler reject since 1.0?\n* returning a slice of a local from the function that owns it\n- returning a slice of a parameter\n- storing a slice of a literal in a global\n> Rule R1 (now V1): the local dies with the function. A parameter's storage belongs to the caller, and a literal lives as long as the program.\n";
static const char nx_str_934[472] = "Q: What does `!allocates` after a return type mean?\n- the function allocates\n* the function promises never to allocate, and the compiler checks it\n- the function frees what it allocated\n> A negative effect bound is a promise the checker enforces through every callee.\n\nQ: Which of these carries the `panics` effect?\n* `a + b` on two `i32` values\n- `a +% b`\n- `a +| b`\n> Checked arithmetic traps on overflow, a defined panic; the wrapping and saturating forms never fail.\n";
